    {
      "slug": "reddit-clock-in-exact-second",
      "script": "Clock in exactly when? My new supervisor sent a memo: no clocking in before your exact scheduled shift time, not even one minute early, per corporate policy. I used to arrive ten minutes early to prep the register and be ready for customers right at nine sharp. Not anymore. I stood outside the door until 8:59, then clocked in the literal second my shift started. That meant the customers who showed up right at opening waited while I did setup on the clock. Within a week, corporate was fielding complaints about the nine a.m. line. My supervisor asked why. I said I was just following her memo, word for word. She tried to reverse it verbally. I asked for that in writing. The memo got officially rescinded, and my setup time became paid. Would you obey that memo?",
      "editor_note": "Rewritten to 138 words with a four-word question hook, every protected detail and shot phrase preserved, and a memo-specific closing question."
    },
    {
      "slug": "reddit-clearance-backpack-coupon",
      "script": "Six free backpacks—seriously? A woman reached my register with a cart full of clearance-tagged backpacks and one expired coupon for buy one backpack, get one free. She had six backpacks stacked up and expected the coupon to repeat through the pile. When I said it covered exactly one pair, she demanded my manager, loudly, and announced she had shopped here for fifteen years. My manager read the coupon out loud, then pointed at the fine print excluding clearance items entirely. Every backpack was clearance. So the coupon covered none of them. She called that rule new. My manager pulled up the same coupon from three months earlier—with identical fine print. Quiet paperwork, undefeated. She bought one backpack at full price and left. The line clapped once the doors closed. Would you have read the coupon first?",
      "editor_note": "Rewritten to 136 words with a four-word question hook, one dry aside, every protected quantity and shot phrase preserved, and a coupon-specific closing question."
    },
